  "unit": "latin",
  "title": "Run, Follow, Walk and Flee: cur, sequ, ped and fug",
  "short": "cur means run, sequ means follow, ped means foot and fug means flee.",
  "trouble": "ped (foot, Latin) is not ped (child, Greek, as in pediatrics). cursory has nothing to do with cursing: a cursory look runs over things.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.con-syn"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "A <i>current</i> runs through a river, and a <i>cursor</i> runs across a screen. <b>cur</b> (also <b>curr</b> and <b>curs</b>) means “run.” A <i>sequel</i> follows a story: <b>sequ</b> means “follow.” A <i>pedal</i> is pushed with your foot: <b>ped</b> means “foot.” A <i>fugitive</i> flees: <b>fug</b> means “flee.”"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "stadium",
            "cast": [
              {
                "who": "maya",
                "x": 38,
                "pose": "run",
                "mood": "determined",
                "face": "right"
              },
              {
                "who": "jamal",
                "x": 66,
                "pose": "run",
                "mood": "joy",
                "face": "right"
              }
            ],
            "fx": [
              {
                "kind": "motion",
                "on": "c0",
                "at": "body",
                "dir": -1
              },
              {
                "kind": "motion",
                "on": "c1",
                "at": "body",
                "dir": -1
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "CUR = RUN",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "Two runners race around a track."
          }
        },
        "cap": "Two runners race around a track."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>cur</b>, <b>curr</b>, <b>curs</b> = <b>run</b>. <b>sequ</b>, <b>secut</b> = <b>follow</b>. <b>ped</b> = <b>foot</b>. <b>fug</b> = <b>flee</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "These roots of movement show up in surprising places. A <b>cursory</b> glance runs over a page. A <b>precursor</b> runs ahead of what comes later. To <b>expedite</b> is to free the feet. A <b>subterfuge</b> is a trick for fleeing in secret."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "cur",
            "sequ"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "cur (run) and sequ (follow)."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "ped",
            "fug"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "ped (foot) and fug (flee)."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>cursory</b><br>hasty and not thorough",
            "<b>curs</b> <i>run</i> + -ory<br>→ “running (over things quickly)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>precursor</b><br>something that comes before and leads to another",
            "pre- <i>before</i> + <b>curs</b> <i>run</i> + -or <i>one who</i><br>→ “one who runs before”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>recurrent</b><br>happening again and again",
            "re- <i>again</i> + <b>curr</b> <i>run</i> + -ent<br>→ “running again”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>concur</b><br>to agree",
            "con- <i>together</i> + <b>cur</b> <i>run</i><br>→ “run together”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>obsequious</b><br>too eager to please or obey",
            "ob- <i>toward</i> + <b>sequ</b> <i>follow</i> + -ious<br>→ “following toward (too eagerly)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>subsequent</b><br>coming after",
            "sub- <i>next</i> + <b>sequ</b> <i>follow</i> + -ent<br>→ “following next”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>expedite</b><br>to speed up",
            "ex- <i>out</i> + <b>ped</b> <i>foot</i> + -ite<br>→ “free the feet”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>pedestrian</b><br>ordinary and dull",
            "<b>ped</b> <i>foot</i> + -estrian<br>→ “going on foot”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>subterfuge</b><br>trickery used to hide or escape something",
            "subter <i>secretly</i> + <b>fug</b> <i>flee</i> + -e<br>→ “fleeing in secret”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "A walking word",
        "q": "The critic called the movie’s plot <b>pedestrian</b>. Was that praise?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>ped</b> (foot) + <b>-estrian</b>: going on foot, like a walker on a sidewalk.",
          "As an adjective, it pictures writing that plods along on foot instead of taking flight.",
          "So a pedestrian plot is ordinary and dull."
        ],
        "a": "No: pedestrian means ordinary and dull."
      },
      {
